    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "4. Nouns, Articles & Gender"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Intergermanic has three grammatical genders: masculine ( maskulin ), feminine ( feminin ), and neuter ( nøytral ). Gender is largely predictable from natural gender or word endings."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "Definite & Indefinite Articles"
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
        "",
        "Masculine",
        "Feminine",
        "Neuter",
        "Plural (all)"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Indefinite (a / an)",
          "ein",
          "ein / eine",
          "eit / et",
          "—"
        ],
        [
          "Definite (the)",
          "-en / den",
          "-en / die",
          "-et / det",
          "-ene / de"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "The definite article can be attached as a suffix ( -en , -et , -ene ) Scandinavian-style OR used as a separate word ( den , die , det , de ) German/Dutch-style. Both are valid in Intergermanic."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Examples:"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "ein mann / mannen — a man / the man"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "eine frau / frauen — a woman / the woman"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "eit hus / huset — a house / the house"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "de barn / barnen — the children"
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "Noun Gender Guide"
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
        "Gender",
        "Typical endings / categories",
        "Examples"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Masculine",
          "-er, -mann, -en; male persons, days, months",
          "mann, dag, venn, vader"
        ],
        [
          "Feminine",
          "-e, -ing, -heit, -keit; female persons, many abstracts",
          "frau, kvinde, arbeiding, friheit"
        ],
        [
          "Neuter",
          "-um, -ment, -sel; many monosyllabic basic nouns",
          "hus, land, system, barn"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "5. Noun Cases"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Intergermanic uses three cases: Nominative (subject), Accusative (direct object), and Genitive (possession). Case is primarily marked on the article/determiner rather than the noun itself, minimising irregularity."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "Case Declension of Articles"
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
        "Case",
        "Masculine",
        "Feminine",
        "Neuter",
        "Plural"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Nominative (subj.)",
          "ein / den",
          "eine / die",
          "eit / det",
          "— / de"
        ],
        [
          "Accusative (dir. obj.)",
          "einen / den",
          "eine / die",
          "eit / det",
          "— / de"
        ],
        [
          "Genitive (poss.)",
          "eins / dens",
          "einer / dier",
          "eits / dets",
          "— / der"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Accusative is identical to Nominative for feminine, neuter, and plural — only masculine changes ( ein → einen ). This mirrors the widespread pattern across Dutch, German, and Scandinavian."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "Case Usage with Examples"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Nominative — subject of the sentence:"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Den mann seer die frau. — The man sees the woman."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Eit barn leiker i huset. — A child plays in the house."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Accusative — direct object:"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Ik seer einen mann. — I see a man."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Wi hæver det buch. — We have the book."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Han gifft die frau einen blomst. — He gives the woman a flower."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Genitive — possession:"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Huset dens manns er stort. — The man's house is big."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Die farbe dets himmelts er blå. — The colour of the sky is blue."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Den namm eins vennts — A friend's name"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Genitive can also be expressed with the preposition van / av + Nominative — a pattern natural to Dutch and Scandinavian speakers: huset av den mann = \"the house of the man\"."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "6. Pronouns"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Personal pronouns inflect for person, number, and case. Where two forms appear (e.g. mik / meg ), the first is closer to Dutch/German, the second to Scandinavian — both are fully acceptable."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
        "Person",
        "Nominative",
        "Accusative",
        "Genitive (poss.)"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "1st sg. (I)",
          "ik",
          "mik / meg",
          "min / mein"
        ],
        [
          "2nd sg. (you)",
          "du",
          "dik / deg",
          "din / dein"
        ],
        [
          "3rd sg. m. (he)",
          "han",
          "him / ham",
          "hans / sein"
        ],
        [
          "3rd sg. f. (she)",
          "hun",
          "hir / henne",
          "hir / ihr"
        ],
        [
          "3rd sg. n. (it)",
          "det",
          "det",
          "dets / sein"
        ],
        [
          "1st pl. (we)",
          "wi",
          "uns / oss",
          "uns / unser"
        ],
        [
          "2nd pl. (you all)",
          "i / ji / ir",
          "iuk / dik",
          "eur / iuer"
        ],
        [
          "3rd pl. (they)",
          "de",
          "dem / them",
          "dær / ihr"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "7. Verb System"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Intergermanic verbs are conjugated for person, number, tense, aspect, and voice. The infinitive ends in -e (e.g. seje, gåe, wærre, hæbbre ). Conjugation is regular for the vast majority of verbs."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "Personal Endings (Present Tense)"
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
        "Person",
        "Ending",
        "Example: seje (to see)"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "ik (I)",
          "-∅ / -er",
          "ik sej / sejer"
        ],
        [
          "du (you)",
          "-er / -st",
          "du sejer / sejst"
        ],
        [
          "han / hun / det (he/she/it)",
          "-er",
          "han sejer"
        ],
        [
          "wi (we)",
          "-e",
          "wi seje"
        ],
        [
          "i / ji (you pl.)",
          "-e",
          "i seje"
        ],
        [
          "de (they)",
          "-e",
          "de seje"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Personal endings are optional when the subject pronoun is stated (Scandinavian style); they are used for clarity when the pronoun is omitted (German/Dutch style)."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "8. Tenses & Aspects"
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "9. Voice"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Intergermanic distinguishes three voices: Active, Passive, and Middle / Neutral (reflexive-reciprocal)."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "9.1 Active Voice ( Aktiv )"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "The default voice. The subject performs the action."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Den mann baut eit hus. — The man builds a house."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Wi seje die stjerne. — We see the stars."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "9.2 Passive Voice ( Passiv )"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Formed with wærre or wirde + past participle. The agent (if expressed) uses van / av ."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Eit hus wirde gebaut. — A house is being built."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Den mann wirde gesejet van die frau. — The man is seen by the woman."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Die buch wærde geschreiben van einem student. — The book was written by a student."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
        "Tense",
        "Passive formation",
        "Example"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Present Passive",
          "wirde + p.p.",
          "wirde gesejet"
        ],
        [
          "Imperfect Passive",
          "wærde + p.p.",
          "wærde gesejet"
        ],
        [
          "Perfect Passive",
          "wærre geworren + p.p.",
          "wærre geworren gesejet"
        ],
        [
          "Future Passive",
          "skal/will wærre + p.p.",
          "skal wærre gesejet"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "9.3 Middle / Neutral Voice ( Medial / Nøytral )"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Covers reflexive, reciprocal, and inherent-process meanings. Formed either with the reflexive particle sik (or seg , Scandinavian style) or with the suffix -st on the verb (Scandinavian mediopassive)."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Ik wæsje sik. — I wash myself. (reflexive)"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Wi seje sik evri dag. — We see each other every day. (reciprocal)"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Die dør opnest. — The door opens (by itself). (mediopassive)"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Het hus bautst raskt. — The house builds quickly / is built quickly."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "The -st mediopassive is closest to Scandinavian s-passives (Norw. læres , Sw. lärs ) and feels natural to Nordic speakers. The sik particle feels natural to German and Dutch speakers."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "10. Mood"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Three moods: Indicative (reality), Conditional (hypothetical), and Imperative (commands)."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "10.1 Indicative"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "The default mood for statements of fact. All tenses in §8 are indicative by default."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "10.2 Conditional"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Used for hypothetical, counterfactual, and polite statements. Modal wulde / wølde (\"would\") + infinitive for Conditional I; wulde hæbbe + past participle for Conditional II."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Conditional I — Present/Future Hypothetical"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Ik wulde gåe, wenn ik hædde tid. — I would go if I had time."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Du wuldest wærre glad, wenn du kæmde. — You would be happy if you came."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Wi wulde kauppe eit hus, wenn wi hædde penger. — We would buy a house if we had money."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Conditional II — Past Hypothetical (Unrealised)"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Ik wulde hæbbe gesejet ihm. — I would have seen him."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "De wulde hæbbe gekommend, wenn de hædde gewust. — They would have come if they had known."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Conditional in subordinate clauses (Konjunktiv II equivalent)"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "In if-clauses ( wenn / om ), the past subjunctive is used. For most verbs this is identical to the imperfect. For wærre and hæbbe , special subjunctive forms exist:"
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
        "Verb",
        "Present Indicative",
        "Past Subj. / Conditional Protasis"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "wærre (to be)",
          "ik wærre",
          "ik wære / wærre"
        ],
        [
          "hæbbe (to have)",
          "ik hæbbe",
          "ik hædde"
        ],
        [
          "moge (may)",
          "ik mog",
          "ik møgte / möchte"
        ],
        [
          "kunne (can)",
          "ik kann",
          "ik kønde / könnte"
        ],
        [
          "skulle (shall)",
          "ik skal",
          "ik skulde / sollte"
        ],
        [
          "wille (to want)",
          "ik will",
          "ik wulde / wollte"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "10.3 Imperative"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Commands use the bare verb stem (identical to 2nd sg. without pronoun). Plural commands add -e ."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Sej! / Seje wi! — See! / Let's see!"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Komm! / Komme! — Come! (sg.) / Come! (pl.)"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Gåe nicht so raskt! — Don't go so fast!"
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "11. Modal Auxiliaries"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Modal verbs take the infinitive without any additional particle. Negation uses nicht directly before the modal or the infinitive: Ik kann nicht seje det or Ik nicht kann seje det ."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
        "Modal",
        "Meaning",
        "Example"
      ],
      "rows": [
