        [
          "Genitive",
          "dens goden manns",
          "dier goden frau",
          "dets goden huses",
          "der goden menner"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "12.3 Comparison of Adjectives"
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
        "Degree",
        "Formation",
        "Example: stor (big)"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Positive",
          "base form",
          "stor"
        ],
        [
          "Comparative",
          "stem + -er / -re",
          "størr / større"
        ],
        [
          "Superlative",
          "stem + -est / -st",
          "størst"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Example: eit stort hus — eit større hus — det største hus (a big house — a bigger house — the biggest house)."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "13. Word Order & Syntax"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Basic order: SVO (Subject-Verb-Object), identical to Scandinavian and similar to Dutch. Subordinate clauses move the verb to the end (V-final), mirroring German and Dutch syntax."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "13.1 Main Clause (SVO)"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Ik sej den mann. — I see the man."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Die frau kauppt eit buch. — The woman buys a book."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Wi gåe til den by morgend. — We go to the city tomorrow."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "13.2 V2 Rule (Verb-Second)"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "When a non-subject starts the sentence, the verb still comes second (inverted). V2 is shared by all continental Germanic and Scandinavian languages."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Morgend gåe wi til den by. — Tomorrow (we) go to the city."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Dit hæbbe ik nicht gesejet. — That I have not seen."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "13.3 Subordinate Clause (SOV with dass/at )"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "In clauses introduced by complementisers ( dass / at / dat ) or relative pronouns, the verb moves to the end:"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Ik weit, dass han kommt. — I know that he comes."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Hun sagte, at de nicht kæmde. — She said that they did not come."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Ik gåe, wen du kommst. — I go when you come."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "13.4 Negation"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Negation uses nicht / ikke placed after the finite verb in main clauses, and before the main verb in subordinate clauses."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Ik sej nicht den mann. — I do not see the man."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Ik weit, dass han nicht kommt. — I know that he does not come."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Wi hæbbe nicht gegåen. — We have not gone."
    },
